 CODE
Art. 2.3.22. 
Common property 
The following are common assets
1¡ the income from the professional activity of each of the spouses, any income or compensation in lieu thereof or in addition thereto, as well as income from the exercise of public or private mandates; the indemnity in lieu of notice and other benefits to which a spouse is entitled by reason of the termination of his or her employment contract, for the portion thereof corresponding to the period of notice running during the regime; 
2¡ the fruits, income and interest of their own property; 
3¡ property given or bequeathed to both spouses jointly or to one of them with the stipulation that such property is to be held in common; 
4¡ compensation paid to a spouse as reparation for damage, insofar as such compensation is intended to make good his or her domestic or economic incapacity during the regime; 
5¡ the property value of the company shares referred to in Article 2.3.19, ¤ 1er , 5¡; 
6¡ the asset value of professional assets acquired by one of the spouses with joint funds, if the right to these professional assets is specific by virtue of Article 2.3.19, ¤ 1er , 6¡; 
7¡ the economic value of the clientele which has been constituted or acquired during the regime by one of the spouses in the exercise of his profession or the operation of his business, if the right to this clientele is proper by virtue of Article 2.3.19, ¤ 1er , 7¡.
¤ 2 An insurance benefit linked to an individual life insurance contract concluded by one of the spouses during the regime is also common if it is due to one of the spouses during the regime. If the benefit is paid in the form of a lump sum, the entire amount is shared. If the benefit is paid in the form of an annuity, the amounts of the annuity paid during the regime as well as the reserve corresponding to the annuities still due after the dissolution of the regime are common.
¤ 3 Finally, all property which is not proven to be the property of one of the spouses by application of a provision of the law is also joint property.
